<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Internal Macedonian Revolutionary Organization</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1893">1893</a>–<a href="/years/1934">1934</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as IMRO, Internal Macedonian-Adrianople Revolutionary Organisation</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Internal Macedonian Revolutionary Organization was a secret revolutionary society founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a> in 1893 to win autonomy for Macedonia and the Adrianople region from Ottoman rule. In 1903 it launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilinden-preobrazhenie-uprising">Ilinden Uprising</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-region">Macedonia</a>, during which rebels proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krusevo">Kruševo</a> Republic, and the Preobrazhenie Uprising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, which created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strandzha">Strandzha</a> Commune. Both were crushed by Ottoman troops. The organisation later split into factions and was suppressed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in 1934.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/internal-macedonian-revolutionary-organization.jpg" alt="Internal Macedonian Revolutionary Organization" width="320" height="337" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maxim Bluecher · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IMRO_badge.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Internal Macedonian Revolutionary Organization, IMRO, was a secret revolutionary society founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Salonica</a> in 1893. It sought autonomy for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-region">Macedonia</a> and the Adrianople region within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. Its founders were Hristo Tatarchev, Dame Gruev, Petar Pop-Arsov, Andon Dimitrov, Hristo Batandzhiev and Ivan Hadzhinikolov, all connected with the Bulgarian Men's High School of Thessaloniki. They modelled it on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasil-levski">Vasil Levski</a>'s earlier Bulgarian revolutionary organisation and took its motto, Freedom or Death. Its first statute of 1894 reserved membership for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>, but Gotse Delchev had it opened to all inhabitants of European Turkey and sought autonomy under the slogan Macedonia for the Macedonians.</p><p>The organisation remained divided over aims from the start. Some members saw autonomy leading to an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-region">Macedonia</a> within a Balkan federation, others to union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. In practice its base stayed among Slavic speakers, primarily Bulgarian Exarchists, and it was often called the Bulgarian Committee.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After a Foreign Representation was set up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a> in 1896, the organisation fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> tactics, in places running a state within a state with its own tax collectors. The Ottoman discovery of an ammunition depot in 1897, the Vinitsa Affair, led to wide repression and turned it into a militant guerrilla force whose bands were called chetas. In 1903 the struggle escalated into the Ilinden-Preobrazhenie Uprising, in which about 15,000 irregulars faced about 40,000 Ottoman soldiers for over seven weeks. The Ottomans destroyed some 100 villages, and afterwards IMRO turned to more systematic terrorism against civilians.</p><p>The defeat split it into a left-wing federalist faction, led by Yane Sandanski, who favoured autonomy and a Balkan Federation, and a right-wing centralist faction, led by figures such as Ivan Garvanov and Boris Sarafov, who favoured union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Fighting between the factions reached assassinations: in 1907 Todor Panitsa, on Sandanski's instructions, killed Sarafov and Garvanov. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the organisation supported the Bulgarian army. After the Treaty of Neuilly the movement separated into Macedonian and Thracian organisations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> claimed the heroes of the early IMRO, above all Delchev and Sandanski, and from 1948 to 1989 disputes over Macedonian history between them followed their political relations. Both states called the interwar leaders Todor Aleksandrov and Ivan Mihailov fascist. A new party using the name, VMRO-DPMNE, was founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a> on 17 June 1990 and claims descent from the old organisation, though there is no real connection between them. A separate party, VMRO, was also revived in Bulgaria after 1989.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Internal Macedonian Revolutionary Organization” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 2 August 1903, the feast of Saint Elijah, the <b>Internal Macedonian-Adrianople Revolutionary Organisation</b> launched the Ilinden Uprising against Ottoman rule in Macedonia.</blockquote><a href="/state/krusevo-republic">Kruševo Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>In August 1903, alongside the Ilinden Uprising in Macedonia, the Adrianople revolutionary district of the <b>Internal Macedonian-Adrianople Revolutionary Organisation</b> launched the Preobrazhenie Uprising in eastern Thrace, on the Feast of the Transfiguration.</blockquote><a href="/state/strandzha-commune">Strandzha Commune</a></li></ul></section>
